Hi Dad,

The senior apartments need proof of how much Social Security pays you each
month. There is one specific document that does this, and I found out exactly
how to get it.

THE DOCUMENT IS CALLED A "BENEFIT VERIFICATION LETTER"

That is Social Security's official name for it. Some places call it a "proof
of income letter," a "budget letter," an "award letter," or a "benefits
letter." Those are all the SAME thing. If an apartment asks for any of those
names, this is the document.

It shows your name, your date of birth, and the exact dollar amount Social
Security pays you every month. That is precisely what the apartments want.

You do NOT already need an account or a login. You can set one up yourself,
free, in about ten minutes, and then download the letter as a PDF right then
and there. That is the fastest way by far. Instructions below.

If the computer part is frustrating, skip to OPTION 2 — one phone call, and
they mail it to you. Either one works. Do whichever is easier for you.


==========================================================================
OPTION 1 - ONLINE (BEST - you get the PDF immediately, same day)
==========================================================================

Have these ready before you start:

  * Your Social Security number
  * Your driver's license (it must NOT be expired)
  * Your date of birth
  * An email address you can check
  * A cell phone that can receive a text message
  * A computer or phone with a camera

Step 1.  Go to this website:   www.ssa.gov/myaccount

Step 2.  Click "Sign In" or "Create an Account."

Step 3.  It will ask you to sign in through a service called LOGIN.GOV.
         That is normal - Social Security stopped using its own passwords.
         Choose Login.gov. (If Login.gov gives you trouble, there is a
         second choice called ID.me that does the same job.)

Step 4.  Make the account: enter your email, create a password, and they
         will text a security code to your phone. Type in that code.

Step 5.  Verify who you are. It will ask you to:
           - take a photo of the front and back of your driver's license
           - take a selfie (a photo of your face)
           - enter your Social Security number and date of birth
         This part takes 5 to 30 minutes. Go slow, it is fine.

Step 6.  Once you are signed in to Social Security, look on the RIGHT SIDE
         of the page for a blue link that says "Replacement Documents."
         Click it.

Step 7.  Click "Get a Benefit Verification Letter."

Step 8.  Make sure the MONTHLY BENEFIT AMOUNT is included in the letter,
         then click "Apply to Letter."

Step 9.  Click "Save a Copy." That downloads the PDF to your computer.

Step 10. Email that PDF to me at Bryan@thestarbucks.com and I will get it
         to the apartments.

That is it. No waiting, no mail.


==========================================================================
OPTION 2 - BY PHONE (easiest, but they mail it - about 10 business days)
==========================================================================

Call:  1-800-772-1213     (TTY: 1-800-325-0778)

The automated system answers 24 hours a day, 7 days a week. When it asks
what you need, just say:  "PROOF OF INCOME"

It will ask you to confirm your name, your Social Security number, and your
date of birth. That takes about 4 minutes. Then they mail the letter.

If you want to talk to a real person instead, call the same number Monday
through Friday, 8:00 a.m. to 7:00 p.m.

ONE IMPORTANT WARNING: they mail it to whatever address Social Security has
on file for you - not to an address you give them on the call. Since your
driver's license says Redmond, Washington but you are living in San Jacinto,
please make sure with them which address they have on file, or the letter
will go to the wrong place. Ask them straight out: "What address do you have
on file for me?"


==========================================================================
OPTION 3 - IN PERSON (if you would rather just go talk to someone)
==========================================================================

Social Security Administration
3095 W Devonshire Ave
Hemet, CA 92545

  - Half a block east of Sanderson (Hwy 79), one block north of
    Florida Ave (Hwy 74)
  - Open Monday through Friday, 9:00 a.m. to 4:00 p.m. Closed weekends.
  - Local office phone: 1-855-207-7083

CALL FIRST FOR AN APPOINTMENT: 1-800-772-1213, weekdays 7 a.m. to 7 p.m.
Since 2025 they run on appointments. They will still help you if you walk
in without one, but you will wait a lot longer (about 26 minutes versus
about 6 minutes with an appointment).

Bring your driver's license.

BONUS: if you go in person, they can also verify your identity right there,
which unlocks the online account for you. After that, any time you need this
letter again you can just download it in two minutes instead of driving over.


==========================================================================
IF SOMETHING GOES WRONG
==========================================================================

If the online identity check FAILS:
  This happens a lot, and it is usually the address. Your driver's license
  says Redmond, Washington, but you live in San Jacinto, California, and
  their computer notices the mismatch. It is not you doing anything wrong.
  Three things to try:
    1. Try ID.me instead of Login.gov.
    2. Login.gov can verify you at a POST OFFICE. Start online, pick the
       in-person option, and they will email you a barcode plus a list of
       the 10 nearest participating post offices. Bring the barcode and
       your unexpired driver's license. You have 30 days to go.
    3. Just use Option 2 (phone) or Option 3 (in person) above.

Login.gov has its own help line, open 24 hours a day:
  1-844-875-6446   (TTY: 711)

Social Security's main help line:
  1-800-772-1213, weekdays 8:00 a.m. to 7:00 p.m.

And of course, call me. I am happy to sit on the phone with you and walk
through the website together, or do the computer part while you read me the
codes. Do not spend an hour fighting with it alone - just call me.


==========================================================================
ONE LAST THING
==========================================================================

While you are logged in, it is worth also saving:
  * Your SSA-1099 (the annual tax form) - some landlords accept it too
  * Your COLA notice (the yearly cost-of-living increase letter)

Send me whatever you get and I will file it and handle the applications.

Love you, Dad. This is the last piece the apartments are waiting on.

- Bryan
  Bryan@thestarbucks.com

